- decided_at: 2026-09-23T01:54:02+00:00
  event: initial
  value: Docker container backend is the first isolated execution backend: one container per scope, workspace bind-mounted at the canonical host tree, docker exec per command. Prerequisite seam landed first: execution.Backend/BackendScope/ScopeSpec/Launch, Manager.Register, Options.Backend name selection, and RequireIsolation refusing unknown, unisolated, or unavailable backends with no fallback. Scope keeps all authority (policy, command shape, stdin, dir resolution, timeout clamp, process ID) before any backend sees a launch.
  reason: A container contains descendants via the PID namespace, which the local backend explicitly cannot guarantee, and expresses filesystem/network/pids/memory limits as flags instead of three hand-rolled OS implementations. Native primitives would mean AppContainer on Windows, seccomp/landlock on Linux, and deprecated sandbox-exec on macOS - three chances to advertise a boundary weaker than it is. A bind mount keeps Scope.Workspace the host path, so internal/files needs no adapter and there is no copy-back or host-edit conflict detection. Docker Desktop is installed on the dev machine; WSL2 alone was rejected as Windows-only with weak per-run isolation.
- decided_at: 2026-09-23T02:23:49+00:00
  event: superseded-previous
  value: Native pure-Go isolated backends, no Docker and no new dependencies: Windows AppContainer (CreateProcess with SECURITY_CAPABILITIES, zero capabilities so no network) layered on the existing suspended-start Job Object; Linux Landlock plus user/mount/pid/net namespaces; macOS Seatbelt via the built-in sandbox-exec. Windows first. The Backend seam (Backend/BackendScope/ScopeSpec/Launch, RequireIsolation with no fallback, all admission in Scope.Start) is unchanged.
  reason: User rejected Docker: too large, requires installation, and cuts against fastllm being a quick single Go binary. A pure-Go probe on this machine showed an AppContainer launched from Go with pipe-captured output denied reads of the user profile, denied writes outside the workspace, had no DNS/network, and could go run inside a granted workspace. golang.org/x/sys is already a dependency and Windows already contains descendants via Job Objects. Restricted-token/low-integrity was rejected because it only contains writes, so it could not honestly report Isolated.
- decided_at: 2026-09-23T03:01:38+00:00
  event: superseded-previous
  value: Implemented: Windows 'appcontainer' backend in internal/execution. One fixed AppContainer identity fastllm.sandbox with ZERO capabilities (no network, loopback included), process created suspended via windows.CreateProcess with SECURITY_CAPABILITIES + a three-handle PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_HANDLE_LIST, assigned to a kill-on-close Job Object capped at 512 processes. Grants: workspace full-access tree plus the go env GOMODCACHE tree read-execute (covers the auto-selected toolchain); nothing on parent folders. Grants persist, are detected by an ACE scan, and are recorded in the identity folder for RevokeIsolatedBackend. PowerShell shell commands are prefixed with a Workspace: PSDrive rooted at the workspace plus Set-Location. Linux/macOS register no isolated backend.
  reason: Literature review (Lampson 1973, Anderson 1972, Saltzer-Schroeder 1975, Polaris 2006, Provos 2003) pointed to reusing the kernel's reference monitor under a separate principal with deny-by-default and designation-based grants, and away from interposition (Garfinkel 2003, Watson 2007). Probing showed C:\Users is unreadable to a container and cannot be re-permissioned without admin; the isolatedWin32-volumeRootMinimal/profilesRootMinimal capabilities that would fix it are rejected by CreateProcess (ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER) for a plain AppContainer. The PSDrive avoids PowerShell's root-down path walk, so no ancestor grants are needed at all. Containment tests each pair with a local-backend control and all fail when the container attribute is removed.
- decided_at: 2026-09-23T03:35:40+00:00
  event: superseded-previous
  value: Windows 'appcontainer' backend: one fixed zero-capability AppContainer identity (no network, loopback included), suspended CreateProcess into a kill-on-close Job Object capped at 512 processes. Grants only the workspace (full, tree) and the go env GOMODCACHE tree (read-execute, covers the auto-selected toolchain). Commands start in a drive letter mapped to the workspace (DefineDosDevice, pushed per scope and popped on close, shared across scopes/processes by stacking); no grants on any folder above the workspace and no administrator step. The model is told commands see the workspace as that drive. Opt-in via /set sandbox and -sandbox; -revoke-sandbox removes all grants (elevating only to remove a legacy C:\Users grant) and the identity.
  reason: Measured on a controlled tree: PowerShell needs read-attributes and git/MSYS needs list+traverse+read-attributes on every parent directory of the working directory. Under C:\Users that would require an admin grant on C:\Users and would expose the names of everything in the home folder. The isolatedWin32 capabilities are rejected for plain AppContainers. A drive root has no parents, so the drive letter gives full PowerShell and git functionality (incl. stash create for checkpoints) with strictly less exposure; the user chose it over the list-grant option. Negative control: launching at the host path instead of the drive fails git and PowerShell even with the earlier grants present.
